  "worked_solution": "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=5\\operatorname{cos}\u2061X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi ,$ where $n$ is any integer.",
